{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-synthesis-discipline", "file": "references/session-heuristics.md", "content": "## Session heuristic: repeated demo requests without testable hypotheses

When demo outputs use different palettes, orientations, and grid sizes, do not react to each pair with a fresh speculative identity or marker story. Record complete coordinate diffs and normalize salient structures (edge guide, interior markers, filled spans) to separate selection from operation. Each next request should discriminate a stated pair of candidate rules. A demo request that does not change the candidate set is likely wasted effort.

A pre-existing task file may be a placeholder rather than a verified transform. Inspect and execute it as protocol requires, but do not let its existence anchor later reasoning. Preserve a functional candidate while exploring; do not replace it with no-op code. Behavioral verification means exact full-grid reproduction of every supplied pair, not successful import, lint, or execution on the test alone.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790687614/attempts/9048647a014d435d8ae09727a7b7c5b6/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-demo-synthesis-discipline/references/session-heuristics.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-synthesis-discipline", "file": "references/partial-stripe-regression.md", "content": "# Partial stripe completion: evidence and verification

When demos show a partial horizontal stripe whose trailing background becomes marker color 1, first record exact changed coordinates and compare all examples. Distinguish completion to the grid edge from completion to a local boundary, and check full-width stripes for exemption. A separate demo with multi-row patterns may share the same edge-extension operation but can reveal different object-selection conditions; do not assume every non-background row qualifies.

Before submitting, run the candidate transform against each complete demo input and compare the entire output matrix, including cells that did not change. A synthetic test fixture that reproduces only the test's salient stripe is not evidence that the learned rule fits the demonstrations. Then run the same transform on the actual test input and submit exactly that returned grid.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790687614/attempts/9048647a014d435d8ae09727a7b7c5b6/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-demo-synthesis-discipline/references/partial-stripe-regression.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-stream-action-discipline", "file": "references/guide-line-placement-inference.md", "content": "# Inferring added guide-parallel lines

For sparse grids with a long monochrome guide and a small attached patch, compare the complete demo coordinates rather than labeling the new line vaguely as “next to” the guide. Record the guide column, the patch's occupied columns, the output-only line column, and which patch edge faces away from the guide. A useful candidate hypothesis is that the added full-length line occupies the next column beyond the patch's outer edge, while every original cell—including the patch and guide—remains unchanged.

