{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-regression-discipline", "file": "references/marker-gap-axis-caution.md", "content": "# Marker-gap filling: axis and selection are separate hypotheses

A demo that inserts a paint color into single-cell gaps between same-color markers in a horizontal stripe establishes a local pattern for that observed axis. It does not by itself establish that the same interpolation applies vertically, that arbitrary aligned pairs should be completed, or that all cells between the row's extreme markers qualify.

When evaluating sparse repeated markers, compare these candidate masks independently:

- fill each one-cell gap between successive markers;
- fill all background cells between the extreme markers;
- extend markers as rays or crosshairs;
- apply the observed operation along one axis only versus both axes.

Preserve original markers and untouched regions unless the complete demos show otherwise. A hypothesis must explain all changed and unchanged cells in every pair. After rejection, discard the whole prediction and request a discriminating example when candidate outputs differ. Never infer a two-axis rule solely by rotating the interpretation of a single-axis demonstration.

Before submission, run the exact task transform on literal demo fixtures and the test. Verify fixture row counts and widths as well as complete matrix equality; successful code execution on a manually mis-sized grid is not evidence.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790687614/attempts/9048647a014d435d8ae09727a7b7c5b6/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-demo-regression-discipline/references/marker-gap-axis-caution.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-stream-solving", "file": "references/valid-grid-submission.md", "content": "# Reliable grid submission

ARC stream tasks may label dimensions inconsistently or provide long grids where a hand-copied submission can be malformed. Keep the displayed test input in a programmatic representation, invoke the task's `transform(grid)`, and submit exactly the emitted result rather than manually retyping it. Validate that the result is a nonempty rectangular grid, each dimension is within 1–30, and each cell is an integer from 0 to 9.

Treat each demonstration as evidence, not a complete specification. An identity output can coexist with transformations in other instances or regions; compare multiple pairs before concluding the task is a no-op. Before a submission, run the program against every available demo and check exact equality. Do not save a placeholder as if it were a learned transform or claim verification unless it happened.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790687614/attempts/9048647a014d435d8ae09727a7b7c5b6/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-stream-solving/references/valid-grid-submission.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-regression-discipline", "file": "references/local-gap-selection.md", "content": "## Local-gap selection diagnostic

Sparse marker-to-paint examples need exact changed-cell masks, including aligned gaps that stay unchanged. Compare immediate horizontal/vertical bracketing, two-step bracketing, filling every intervening gap, and boundary-only hypotheses. Collinearity by itself does not establish that all intervening background cells are painted. Record marker and paint roles separately; do not infer either from color frequency alone when a demonstration contains multiple foreground colors. Keep literal fixtures and require full-grid equality before submitting a rule across varied demo geometries. A succession of rejected test predictions is a signal to rebuild the selection hypothesis, not to tweak the last output.
